{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:9454332 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:9454332 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"9454332","source":"MED","pmid":"9454332","doi":"10.1126/science.279.5349.349","title":"Extension of life-span by introduction of telomerase into normal human cells.","authorString":"Bodnar AG, Ouellette M, Frolkis M, Holt SE, Chiu CP, Morin GB, Harley CB, Shay JW, Lichtsteiner S, Wright WE.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bodnar AG","lastName":"Bodnar","firstName":"A G","initials":"AG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Geron Corporation, Menlo Park, CA 94025, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ouellette M","lastName":"Ouellette","firstName":"M","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7265-6779"}},{"fullName":"Frolkis M","lastName":"Frolkis","firstName":"M","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Holt SE","lastName":"Holt","firstName":"S E","initials":"SE"},{"fullName":"Chiu CP","lastName":"Chiu","firstName":"C P","initials":"CP"},{"fullName":"Morin GB","lastName":"Morin","firstName":"G B","initials":"GB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8949-4374"}},{"fullName":"Harley CB","lastName":"Harley","firstName":"C B","initials":"CB"},{"fullName":"Shay JW","lastName":"Shay","firstName":"J W","initials":"JW"},{"fullName":"Lichtsteiner S","lastName":"Lichtsteiner","firstName":"S","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Wright WE","lastName":"Wright","firstName":"W E","initials":"WE"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7265-6779"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8949-4374"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5349","volume":"279","journalIssueId":1100470,"dateOfPublication":"1998 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":1998,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Science","title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","essn":"1095-9203","issn":"0036-8075","isoabbreviation":"Science","nlmid":"0404511"},"printPublicationDate":"1998-01-01"},"pubYear":"1998","pageInfo":"349-352","abstractText":"Normal human cells undergo a finite number of cell divisions and ultimately enter a nondividing state called replicative senescence. It has been proposed that telomere shortening is the molecular clock that triggers senescence. To test this hypothesis, two telomerase-negative normal human cell types, retinal pigment epithelial cells and foreskin fibroblasts, were transfected with vectors encoding the human telomerase catalytic subunit. In contrast to telomerase-negative control clones, which exhibited telomere shortening and senescence, telomerase-expressing clones had elongated telomeres, divided vigorously, and showed reduced straining for beta-galactosidase, a biomarker for senescence. Notably, the telomerase-expressing clones have a normal karyotype and have already exceeded their normal life-span by at least 20 doublings, thus establishing a causal relationship between telomere shortening and in vitro cellular senescence. The ability to maintain normal human cells in a phenotypically youthful state could have important applications in research and medicine.","affiliation":"Geron Corporation, Menlo Park, CA 94025, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIA NIH HHS","acronym":"AG","grantId":"AG05747","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIA NIH HHS","acronym":"AG","grantId":"AG07992","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pigment Epithelium of Eye","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Tumor Cells, Cultured"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descripto